// queue): Inframan installs the chart, and the person never runs Helm. Call
// preview_chart_application first, with the same manifest, and show the person
// what it would make and what it refuses.
//
// `manifest` is inframan.yaml's schema for this one application with a `chart:`
// section in place of `services:`:
// version: v1
// application: nats
// chart:
// repository: https://nats-io.github.io/k8s/helm/charts # https://… or oci://…,
// public
// name: nats
// version: 1.3.7 # exact; never a range or latest
// timeout_minutes: 10 # optional, 2–30: how long it may take to be ready
// node_pool: application-pool # only where the infrastructure has several
// values: {...} # the chart's own values, as its README documents them
// exposes: # what services connect to
// NATS_URL: {service: nats, port: 4222, url:
// "nats://app:{password}@{host}:{port}",
// password_from: {secret: nats-auth, key: password}}
// secrets: # infrastructure secrets, by NAME
// NATS_PASSWORD: nats-admin-password
// objects: # an operator chart's: what the operator runs
// - {apiVersion: kafka.strimzi.io/v1beta2, kind: Kafka, metadata: {name:
// events}, spec: {...}}
// It names no alert group: a chart application has no alerts to send one, so a
// `notify:` is refused (CHART_APPLICATION_NOTIFY); its installs' outcomes are
// get_chart_release's and its activity feed's.
//
// `exposes` names a Service and a port the chart makes — or, on an operator
// chart, one its operator makes from objects: (Strimzi's
// <cluster>-kafka-bootstrap), which then needs `protocol` or `url` and is
// checked once the application is ready; the value is host:port (protocol tcp),
// http://host:port (http), https://host:port (https) — unset, what the Service
// port says: its appProtocol, else its name alone or before a dash, http for
// http, http2 and h2c, https for https, tcp otherwise — or `url` with {host},
// {port} and {password} (read from a Secret the chart keeps, password_from). A
// service of any application on the infrastructure connects with `NATS_URL:
// chart/nats/NATS_URL` in its connections: block; the value reaches its pod by
// secretKeyRef and never leaves the cluster. `secrets` name infrastructure
// secrets, never values: they are written into the Secret inframan-secrets in
// its namespace, which a chart's own existingSecret options read (point them at
// that name), and ${secret:NAME} in the values puts one in where a chart has no
// such option. A version's secrets reach that Secret only as its install
// starts; an upgrade that fails gives back what it held. Never write a password
// into the values.
//
// An operator chart — one that asks for powers across the cluster
// (preview_chart_application's `powers`) — waits at awaiting_approval until an
// admin of the instance approves them in the console; tell the person, and do
// not save it again. `objects` are namespaced documents of the application's
// namespace (the cluster the operator runs), installed with the chart and
// waited for. Inframan places the pods of the chart's own templates on the node
// pool as it renders them, and the pods an operator makes from `objects` as no
// node takes them: each is given the node pool's toleration (every node pool is
// tainted), and nothing else of it changes; nothing need be written into an
// object's pod template for it. An admission webhook the operator registers as
// it runs is kept off the platform's namespaces as the chart's own are. A pod
// the cluster refuses the toleration fails the install at once, saying which
// and what to give its object's pod template.
//
// It is kept as version 1 and installed: the application reads installing, then
// ready once every workload it made, every pod of its namespace and every
// object of `objects` that reports a Ready condition is ready (and none reports
// NotReady, Stalled or Reconciling), or failed with the reason (the pod that
// crashed and its log, the pod no node took, the image that would not pull).
// Follow it with get_chart_release. Its pods run on the application node pool's
// nodes; it sleeps and wakes with the infrastructure; get_logs reads its logs
// by the application's name; its images are scanned as it installs and listed
// under it (get_infrastructure_vulnerabilities with application_id); the
// ServiceMonitors and PodMonitors the chart ships are scraped (query_metrics).
//
// It has no public address. Change it later with save_application_manifest
// (each save is a version, and a failed change leaves the last good version
// running).
//
