// CreatePipeline calls create_pipeline: POST /v1/capabilities/pipelines.
//
// Create a CI/CD pipeline on an infrastructure from a YAML definition.
//
// Pipelines are stored by the platform, not committed to the repo, so this is
// how one comes into existence.
//
// Pass the definition as YAML — the same shape export_pipeline_yaml exports, so
// the way to learn the format is to read an existing pipeline first.
//
// Required: `name` and `trigger_event` (push, pull_request or
// pull_request_merged). `trigger_branch` is a glob and defaults to `*`.
//
// Creating a pipeline does not run it, and does not attach it to anything — use
// link_pipeline to bind it to an application.
//
// A pipeline is written in any of three formats, all the same pipeline:
// `inframan` (Inframan YAML, the default: the shape export_pipeline_yaml
// returns), `github` (GitHub Actions workflow syntax: a step is a job, its
// commands the job's run: steps, the phases where jobs sit around two fixed
// jobs `build` and `deploy` — `uses: inframan/build@v1` and `uses:
// inframan/deploy@v1` — and what GitHub has no word for under an `inframan:`
// key; any workflow written for GitHub's runners converts too: checkout,
// caches, docker build/push, cloud sign-ins and deploys are Inframan's own and
// replaced, setup-node/python/go/java choose the step's image, a matrix is
// expanded, a notify step or stage is a job of one `uses: inframan/notify@v1`
// step with its settings under `with:` (a stage's job carries `inframan:
// {stage: true}`), a job's `${{ secrets.X }}` becomes the step's secret X (an
// infrastructure secret of that name) and its `services:` the pre-build step's
// sidecars, and what a pipeline cannot hold — environment:, unknown actions, a
// second trigger — is reported unsupported, never dropped unsaid) and `json`
// (the console's Visual form, the API's field names). A step's `secrets:` maps
// a variable to the NAME of an infrastructure secret (list_infra_secrets),
// never a value: the step gets it as that variable, masked in its log. A
// pre-build step's `sidecars:` ({name, image, env, command, ready}) are
// containers beside it — a database for its tests — reached at their name or
// localhost, `ready` a command run in one until it succeeds.
// list_repository_workflows reads a repository's .github/workflows to import,
// and convert_pipeline shows what a document becomes before anything is saved.
//
// Webhooks (`webhooks:` list) are notifications. Each: `name` (unique in the
// pipeline), `url` (https:// only; the certificate is verified and redirects
// are not followed), `method` (POST default; PUT, PATCH, GET), `headers` (map),
// `events` — `deploy_success` (per service, once its version is ready) and/or
// `release_success` (once per release, after its traffic has moved; POST only;
// body {event, delivery_id, application, infrastructure_id, revision, images:
// {service: image}}), `vulnerabilities_found` (per service, when its release's
// image scan finds anything at or above the service's security.fail_on — high
// where it has no policy) and/or `scan_blocked` (per service, when its security
// policy refuses the release); both carry {event, delivery_id, application,
// infrastructure_id, service, deployment_id, image, digest, critical, high,
// medium, low, fixable, secrets, blocked, code, message, findings: [IDs]}.
// Optional: `secret` — the NAME of an infrastructure secret
// (list_infra_secrets), never a value; it is read at send time and sent in
// `secret_header` (default Authorization) after `secret_prefix` (default
// "Bearer " in Authorization, otherwise none); `retries` (0-10, default 3,
// attempts after the first, from 10s doubling to 5m; 5xx, 408, 429 and network
// errors are retried); `success_codes` (default any 2xx); `timeout_seconds`
// (1-60, default 10); `exclude_services` (left out of release_success's
// images). There is no copy_images_to: copying images is a promote_images
// release stage. Deliveries are on the application's activity feed as
// webhook_delivered / webhook_failed; a webhook never fails a release.
//
// Release stages (`release_stages:` list) run in order once per release, after
// its traffic has moved (never for a superseded, failed or fixed-traffic
// release), and never fail the release. Each: `id` ([a-z][a-z0-9_], at most 32,
// unique), `name`, `type`, `continue_on_failure` (default false: a failed stage
// skips the ones after it). Types: `promote_images` — `registry` (a path such
// as asia-south1-docker.pkg.dev/project/repo; GCP infrastructures only),
// optional `services` and `exclude_services`; copies each image to
// <registry>/<last repository segment>:<tag> as the infrastructure's cloud
// account (it needs roles/artifactregistry.writer there), digest kept; outputs
// `images` {service: copy}. `webhook` — the webhook keys above but `events`,
// plus optional `body`, a Go text/template over .release {application,
// infrastructure_id, revision, images, delivery_id} and .stages.<earlier id>
// {status, outputs}, with functions `json` and `default` (empty body = that
// data as JSON; a JSON body that does not render valid JSON fails the stage);
// sent with X-Inframan-Event: release_success and X-Inframan-Delivery equal to
// .release.delivery_id; outputs `status_code`, `response`. Example body:
// {"images": {{ json .stages.promote.outputs.images }}, "revision": {{
// .release.revision }}}. `notify` — a message through the instance's
// notifications (below). `wait` — `duration_seconds` (1-86400).
// `manual_judgment` — `prompt`, `timeout_minutes` (1-10080, default 1440); a
// person approves or rejects it in the console (an agent cannot); reject or
// timeout fails it. `publish_files` — `service` (the service whose image holds
// the files: one built FROM scratch with them, never run — scaling off,
// min_replicas 0), `bucket` (a Cloud Storage bucket name, no gs://; GCP
// infrastructures only), optional `prefix` (where the files go; `{version}` in
// it is replaced by the content of `version_file`, a file in the image, which
// it then requires) and `overwrite` (default false: a file the bucket already
// has is left as it is, so a published version never changes); uploads every
// regular file of the release's image of the service as the infrastructure's
// cloud account (it needs roles/storage.objectCreator on the bucket,
// objectAdmin to overwrite); outputs `version`, `bucket`, `prefix`, `files`,
// `published`, `already_there`. list_release_stages reads how a release's
// stages ran.
//
// A notify step sends a message through the instance's notifications
// (list_alert_groups, list_notification_channels). In any phase's steps it is a
// step with `name` and `notify: {group | channels, when, message}` in place of
// image and commands; as a release stage it is `type: notify` with `group` or
// `channels`, `when` and `message`. Exactly one of `group` (an alert group's
// name) and `channels` (1-10 channel names) — a save refuses a group the
// instance does not have (ALERT_GROUP_NOT_FOUND) or a channel
// (CHANNEL_NOT_FOUND). `when`: `success` (the default: the run, or the release,
// got this far), `failure` (it failed before this step, or the release failed)
// or `always`. A failed run still sends its later failure and always steps, and
// a failed release its failure and always stages; a stopped one sends nothing.
// `message` is optional, a Go text/template of at most 2000 characters over
// .application, .infrastructure, .revision, .commit, .commit_message, .branch,
// .started_by, .service, .build, .phase, .step, .status, .reason, .link (a
// stage also .stages.<earlier id> {status, outputs}), built-in functions only;
// one that fails to render sends the default with a note. A notify step takes
// no image, commands, env, secrets, sidecars, shell, working_directory,
// watch_paths or force, and never fails a release: what it sent, or why
// nothing, is on the step's record. A notify step or stage is the only way a
// release's outcome reaches a channel: a failed release is on the bell and the
// feed, and no alert group is sent it by itself, so a release stage with `when:
// failure` or `always` is how people hear of one. An alert group or a channel a
// pipeline names cannot be deleted.
