// GetAgentConnections calls get_agent_connections: GET
// /v1/capabilities/infrastructures/:infrastructure_id/agents/connections.
//
// Read an infrastructure's model connections, and what to enter in Anthropic's
// console to make one.
//
// A model connection is how the infrastructure's agents reach a model: every
// agent calls its infrastructure's agent gateway, which holds the connections
// and adds each one's credential, so no agent holds a key. An infrastructure
// keeps several (`max_connections`), each with a `name`, and one is its
// `default`. An agent's inframan.yaml names the connection its model is called
// through as `model: <connection>/<model>`; an alias (opus, sonnet, haiku,
// fable) or a claude-… ID written alone is called through the default
// connection, and a file that names no model is given the default connection's
// `default_model` (the alias opus where it names none). Returns whether the
// Agents app is `installed` (and its `app_status`), whether any connection is
// `set`, and `connections`, the default first: each with its `provider` —
// `anthropic_federation` (workload identity federation: the gateway exchanges a
// short-lived token its cluster issues, and no key exists anywhere),
// `anthropic_api_key` (an API key kept as an infrastructure secret, named by
// `api_key_secret`, never returned), `openai`, `azure_openai` (its resource's
// endpoint in `base_url`) or `openai_compatible` (any endpoint that speaks
// OpenAI's API, in `base_url`: a provider's, or a model server in this cluster,
// `in_cluster`; with a key or none; `api` chat_completions or responses),
// `gemini` (Google's Gemini API, with a key), `vertex_ai` (Anthropic's and
// Google's models through the infrastructure's own Google Cloud project, as the
// gateway's own identity there, on a Google Cloud infrastructure) or `bedrock`
// (Anthropic's models through the infrastructure's own AWS account, as the
// gateway's own role there, on an AWS infrastructure) — its `format`
// (anthropic, openai, gemini, vertex, bedrock), `prices` (its own, in dollars a
// million tokens: a model not of Anthropic's that neither these nor Inframan's
// list prices price refuses an agent's release, MODEL_PRICE_UNKNOWN), whether
// Inframan's agent runtime can call through it (`runtime_calls`), who set it
// and when, its `last_test` (what the last test_agent_connection of it found),
// and `used_by`: the agents whose release calls through it, which is why it
// cannot be removed while any does. `cloud` is the infrastructure's cloud and
// `providers` what a connection may name on it: the cloud's own connection is
// Vertex AI on Google Cloud and Bedrock on AWS. `federation` is what a person
// enters in Anthropic's console under Workload identity, Connect workload, the
// same for every federation connection of the infrastructure, in the order its
// wizard asks: an issuer of `issuer_type` Kubernetes (never Google Cloud, whose
// rule matches a Google service account) at the cluster's `issuer_url`; the
// rule's match conditions, `namespace` and `service_account` (together the
// `subject` it matches exactly), and its `audience`; one workspace of the
// agents' own; the target's `scope` and `token_lifetime_seconds`; with the
// `steps` in that order. The Console gives back four identifiers (organization,
// federation rule, service account, workspace), none of them a secret.
// `cloud_setup` is the setup of the identity the gateway calls the cloud's own
// models as (the Google service account of a Vertex AI connection, the IAM role
// of a Bedrock one: its `setup` a script the user runs in the project or the
// account). Setting, removing or changing a connection, and choosing the
// default, are CONSOLE operations (the Agents app's page): give the user these
// values and let them make it.
//
// It needs the infrastructure:read permission, changes nothing and is offered
// to agents and service accounts (surfaces: console, cli, agent).
func (c *Client) GetAgentConnections(ctx context.Context, p GetAgentConnectionsParams) (json.RawMessage, error) {
	return c.invoke(ctx, "get_agent_connections", p.args())
}

