// image.
//
// Every release scans the image it deploys, beside the release and never
// stopping it: the infrastructure's own Trivy server matches the image's OS and
// language packages against its vulnerability database, and lists any secret
// baked into the image's layers or config. This reads the scan of the serving
// release, or of the release named by deployment_id.
//
// `status` is scanned, scanning (the release's Scan step has not finished) or
// not_scanned, with `reason` saying why (an image built on builder machines
// before scanning, the infrastructure's Trivy server not ready, a release from
// before scanning). `counts` are of the whole scan; `vulnerabilities` are the
// findings matching the filters, the most severe first, then those with a fix,
// then the highest CVSS score; `total` is how many match, and `next_offset`
// pages on. `database_updated_at` is when the vulnerability database the scan
// matched against was built: a finding published after it shows at a later
// scan.
//
// `signature` says whether the release signed the image, by digest, with the
// infrastructure's own KMS key (status signed, signing or not_signed with
// `reason`; an image that could not be signed fails nothing, and `enforced` is
// true only on a release from before the Image policy app was removed).
// Inframan signs only what it builds into the infrastructure's registry; the
// signature is cosign's, beside the image.
//
// `repository` is the release's repository — the service's build context at the
// commit its image was built from — scanned beside the image: its dependencies'
// vulnerabilities (`target` is the lockfile that pins each), secrets committed
// to git (file and line), and `misconfigurations` of its Dockerfile,
// Kubernetes, Terraform and Helm files (check, file, line and `resolution`, how
// to fix it). It is null for a published image and a release from before
// repositories were scanned; its `status` and `reason` say why one was not
// scanned. Under the service's `security.repository: enforce` its findings can
// refuse a release as the image's do.
//
// How to fix each kind:
// • A language package (pkg_type npm, gomod, pip, …): move it to
// `fixed_version` or later in the manifest that declares it (package.json,
// go.mod, requirements.txt), then release.
// • An OS package (pkg_type alpine, debian, ubuntu, …): use a newer tag of the
// Dockerfile's base image, or upgrade the package in the Dockerfile, then
// release.
// • A finding with no `fixed_version` has no fix yet: there is nothing to bump.
