// InstallAddon calls install_addon: POST
// /v1/capabilities/infrastructures/:infrastructure_id/addons/:app_id/install.
//
// Install a named Redis or Postgres onto an infrastructure.
//
// REAL INFRASTRUCTURE, REAL MONEY. This provisions a GKE node pool through
// Terraform and applies the operator's resources inside the cluster. It takes
// MINUTES, not seconds. On a nonprod AWS infrastructure the install runs on the
// system pool instead of a pool of its own, and a prod AWS one refuses it.
//
// MAY COME BACK HELD RATHER THAN STARTED. If this app tier has no verified
// cloud identity on the infrastructure, the call SUCCEEDS with status
// `awaiting_setup`: the install is recorded with everything the customer chose,
// and nothing is provisioned or billing. It is not an error and the request is
// not lost — do not retry it and do not create a second one.
//
// The identity has to exist before the pod does. It is attached to the pod at
// creation and cannot be added to a running database without rebuilding it,
// which on a datastore means downtime and a data move.
//
// When you get `awaiting_setup`, the sequence is: get_cloud_identity_setup for
// this infrastructure, app and tier — show the customer the commands and ask
// them to run them, since Inframan cannot (creating a service account needs a
// permission it is deliberately not granted) — then verify_cloud_identity, then
// continue_addon_install with the installed_app_id you already have. One-time
// per app tier per infrastructure; later installs of the same tier start
// immediately.
//
// The call returns as soon as the workflow starts. It is NOT done when it
// returns: poll get_installed_app until `status` is ready before telling anyone
// the database is up or wiring a service to it. A failed install reports its
// reason there too.
//
// Ask the user before calling. Confirm the infrastructure, the instance name
// and the size — this provisions billable capacity that runs until someone
// removes it, and removing it is not something you can do.
//
// `instance_name` is required and PERMANENT: several named installs of one app
// can live on one infrastructure, and the name is how they are told apart and
// how services address them. It becomes part of the Kubernetes namespace and
// cannot be changed afterwards, so ask rather than inventing one.
//
// `memory` is optional and applies to BOTH apps, but it does not mean the same
// thing in each, so do not carry a number across from one to the other.
//
// For Redis it is the usable DATASET size: the pod is sized 25% above it for
// background-save headroom. For Postgres it is the POD's memory, and roughly a
// quarter becomes shared_buffers while max_connections is derived from what is
// left — so asking for the same figure buys a smaller working set on Postgres
// than on Redis, and quietly changes how many clients it will accept.
//
// In both cases the NODE is then sized to fit the pod, so a larger memory means
// a larger, costlier machine. Choose with the user, because changing it later
// is refused when the new size needs a different machine.
//
// COST CHANGES AT MACHINE BOUNDARIES, NOT AT MEMORY VALUES, and this is the
// single most useful thing you can tell someone sizing an instance. Each
// install gets its own node, so the bill is the node's — every size that lands
// on the same machine costs the same. On a nonprod infrastructure:
// • Postgres — 256mb, 512mb, 1gb and 2gb ALL land on one e2-medium.
// Recommending 256mb over 2gb saves the user nothing and costs them working set
// and connections (a 256mb Postgres gets 20 connections, a 2gb one 120). The
// next machine starts just above 2gb.
// • Redis — 256mb, 512mb and 1gb land on e2-medium; 2gb crosses to the next
// machine, because the pod is sized 25% above the dataset for fork headroom and
// that is what the node must hold.
// So the same "2gb" is one machine class on Postgres and the next one up on
// Redis. Do not carry a size across engines and assume the price follows.
//
// What that means in practice: when a user asks for the cheapest option, do not
// reach for the smallest number. Find the LARGEST size that stays on the same
// machine as the smallest one — it is the same bill with more database. Only
// recommend crossing a boundary when they actually need what is on the other
// side, and say plainly that it is where the cost step happens. Prod is sized
// differently and starts a machine class higher, so these boundaries are
// nonprod ones.
//
// `persistence` decides whether the instance's data sits on a durable volume
// that outlives its pod, and it is PERMANENT — there is no path to flip a
// running install, because turning persistence off would delete the volume the
// data is on. LEAVE IT UNSET unless the user has actually expressed a
// preference: unset means the app's own default, which is on.
//
// Postgres is persistent whatever you send, and an explicit false is refused
// rather than quietly upgraded. It is a database of record; an ephemeral one
// loses every committed transaction the next time its pod moves, and on a
// single Spot node that is measured in hours.
//
// Postgres is BACKED UP by default, and you should still be careful how you say
// so. Every install archives WAL continuously to its own bucket and takes a
// base backup daily at 03:00 UTC, which is what makes point-in-time recovery
// possible. Use `backup_hour_utc` to move that hour; there is no switch here to
// turn it off, and a database of record should not have one.
//
// What that does NOT yet mean is a restore anybody has performed. The mechanism
// is complete and untested end to end, so answer "backups are running and
// recoverable in principle" rather than "your data is safe" — those are
// different claims and only the first is currently evidenced.
//
// Redis is where the choice is real. Off is a legitimate, cheaper configuration
// for a pure read-through cache: no disk to pay for, and nothing in it that
// cannot be recomputed from the origin. What it costs is the RESTART. An add-on
// pool is a single Spot node, so a preemption brings the instance back EMPTY —
// and every request that would have been a cache hit goes to the database
// behind it at once, a cold-start stampede onto the origin at the moment the
// origin is the only thing left. Choose off only when the user has told you
// what is behind the cache and that it can absorb that; otherwise say nothing
// and take the default.
//
// `tier` is "basic", the only one that exists today. The rule behind it, for
// when there are more: a tier promising replication or failover needs a
// REGIONAL cluster, which is what a prod-tagged infrastructure gets. Nonprod is
// zonal by deliberate cost choice, so an HA tier there would place every
// replica in the primary's zone — costing more and surviving nothing — and is
// refused. Do not invent a tier name: list_platform_apps with an
// infrastructure_id reports `allowed_tiers` per add-on for that specific
