// ListDeployments calls list_deployments: GET /v1/capabilities/deployments.
//
// The CI/CD run feed: recent deployments across the instance, newest first.
//
// Each row carries its service, application and infrastructure, plus status,
// trigger reason, and — on runs that got that far — commit and image. This is
// the fastest way to answer "what happened to my last deploy": read it before
// asking the user for details they have already got.
//
// WHAT THE STATUSES MEAN. PENDING, BUILDING, QUEUED and DEPLOYING are in
// flight. READY means the service is built, applied and its pods are up — and
// NOT released: the release has not promoted it yet, so do not report a READY
// run as shipped. DEPLOYED means released, traffic has moved. SKIPPED means the
// service is fine and takes no traffic, because the release it belonged to
// failed or because nothing it watches changed. FAILED is the run itself.
//
// A RELEASE'S VERDICT IS ITS REVISION'S, not its rows'. Rows of one release can
// read READY while the release is still deciding; if you need to know whether a
// release shipped, read the revision.
//
// commit_hash and image_url are written on SUCCESS. A FAILED run usually has
// both empty even when its build resolved a commit and pushed an image — that
// is recorded in list_application_activity, not here. Empty means the run did
// not reach that step, never that no such commit exists.
//
// commit_hash is the commit the IMAGE was built from, which is not always the
// push that cut the release. For a service with a monorepo path it is the
// newest commit touching THAT PATH, so a commit changing only inframan.yaml at
// the repo root releases the service without touching its directory: the build
// is skipped, the previous image is reused, and commit_hash names the older
// commit that image came from. The commit that triggered the release is in
// trigger_reason.
//
// So: to answer "is my change live", read trigger_reason. To answer "what code
// is in this image", read commit_hash. Quoting one for the other is how a
// config change looks like it never deployed.
//
// Paginated by an opaque cursor, exactly as list_application_activity is. The
// reply `count` is the size of THIS PAGE, not the number of runs that exist —
// there is no total. Follow next_cursor until it comes back empty if you need
// them all.
//
// is an object — {count, deployments, next_cursor} — and next_cursor is how you
// tell a complete answer from a truncated one: non-empty means there ARE more
// runs, so pass it back as cursor and keep going until it comes back empty. An
// empty next_cursor is the only proof you have seen every run matching the
// filter. How many rows came back tells you nothing on its own.
//
// It needs the deployment:read permission, changes nothing and is offered to
// agents and service accounts (surfaces: console, cli, agent).
