// PreviewChartApplication calls preview_chart_application: POST
// /v1/capabilities/chart-applications/preview.
//
// Check a chart application's manifest: render the chart against its cluster
// and list what it would make and what Inframan refuses, installing nothing.
//
// Always call this before create_chart_application, and before saving a changed
// chart application's manifest. It fetches the chart, renders it as Helm would
// install it against the infrastructure's cluster (whose API server answers
// while the infrastructure sleeps), places it on the application node pool, and
// returns: the objects it would create (hooks marked), the workloads with their
// replicas, the disks with their sizes, the images, the chart tests left out,
// `exposes` — each with the value a service connecting to it would be given —
// and `refusals` — what Inframan refuses and why. Any refusal refuses the whole
// chart: fix the values (most charts can turn the refused part off) or choose
// another chart. An expose naming a Service or a port the chart does not make
// is refused, naming the Services and ports it does make. Secrets are put in as
// placeholders (<secret NAME>): Check reads no value.
//
// Refused: objects in another namespace, anything past Inframan's edge (a
// Service of type LoadBalancer or NodePort, an Ingress, Gateway API, Istio or
// Knative kinds), anything that reaches the node (privileged containers, host
// namespaces, hostPath, host ports, added capabilities — Pod Security's
// baseline level), a ServiceMonitor or PodMonitor that reads a file
// (bearerTokenFile, a TLS file), selects another namespace or writes the labels
// Prometheus gives a series (honorLabels; a relabeling or targetLabels of
// namespace, pod, container, node, service, job or instance; a labelmap; a
// rewrite of __address__), a Secret named inframan-secrets… or
// chart-<application>-binding, which Inframan keeps, a ServiceAccount's
// long-lived token Secret, and a resource type of one of Kubernetes' own API
// groups. Refused too: Inframan's own labels and annotations
// (`inframan.cloud/…`, `managed-by: inframan`) on the chart's objects, a
// Service's `externalIPs`, a render too large for Helm to keep its release
// record, and a namespace or Helm release that is not the application's own.
//
// An operator chart's powers across the whole cluster — its resource types
// (CRDs), ClusterRoles and their bindings, admission webhooks and policies, any
// other cluster-wide object — are listed in `powers`, said in words with
// `risks` to read twice, not refused; with them, where its pods carry them,
// `runs` (what each of its pods runs, and as whom, with the data of the
// ConfigMaps and Secrets it reads — the passwords and certificates the chart
// generates left out), `namespace_roles` and `objects` (each object of
// `objects` its operator acts on, with the images, accounts and commands it
// names), which are approved with them. An object of a resource type another
// chart application brings is in `objects` whoever renders it — that
// application's operator acts on it — so a chart holding nothing across the
// cluster needs an approval too when its objects are of such a type. Once the
// application has been approved (`powers.approved_before`), every version is
// approved, one holding nothing across the cluster too: its `runs`, the Secrets
// and ConfigMaps it makes (`config`) and its objects are listed as an operator
// chart's are; and `kept` names the Secrets, ConfigMaps, Roles and RoleBindings
// of its namespace a version no admin approved left (and `runs` marks `kept`
// what it left running, `kept_disks` its disks by name and size), approved as
// they are now, a Secret's values and a disk's contents never shown; `quiesce`
// says approving stops what runs in the namespace now, and replaces its service
// accounts, until the approved version is installed; a pod another operator
// makes there that runs as an account the chart empowers is in `refusals`. A
// version making nothing else to approve is approved as a whole (`whole`,
// `makes`). `approval` is `needed` until an admin of the instance approves them
// on the application's page (an agent cannot), or `approved` where an earlier
// approval covers them all. A resource type the cluster has already under
// another owner — another chart application, the platform — is left as it is
// (listed in `dropped`, and in `resource_types_left`). `notes` say what
// Inframan does not do for it, where there is anything: the pods an operator
// makes from `objects` are not among it, since Inframan gives each that no node
// takes the node pool's toleration.
//
// Nothing is installed and nothing is kept. Give application_id to check a
// change to an existing chart application; give infrastructure_id for a new
// one. The manifest travels in the request's body, as
// create_chart_application's does, so anything that can be created can be
// checked first.
//
// It needs the permission creating a chart application needs
// (application:create), not a read's: it fetches a chart the caller chose and
// renders it against the infrastructure's cluster. A viewer cannot check one.
//
// It needs the application:create permission, changes nothing (a read sent as a
// POST) and is offered to agents and service accounts (surfaces: console, cli,
// agent).
