// ProvisionInfrastructure calls provision_infrastructure: POST
// /v1/capabilities/infrastructures/provision.
//
// Provision a new environment: private GKE cluster, VPC, registry, load
// balancer and the platform stack.
//
// Builds a whole environment inside the customer's own GCP project: a VPC with
// subnet, Cloud NAT and firewall rules; a PRIVATE GKE cluster with the node
// pools you pass plus the platform's own system pool; an Artifact Registry; a
// load balancer; and on top of it a LEAN platform: the Istio ingress/routing
// engine, cert-manager, CI/CD, and Google-managed observability. Knative
// (scale-to-zero) and the in-cluster Prometheus/Loki/Tempo stack are platform
// apps installed afterwards from the infrastructure's Apps tab when wanted, not
// part of provisioning.
//
// THIS CREATES REAL, BILLABLE CLOUD RESOURCES. A cluster with a couple of
// always-on nodes costs money every hour it exists, and nothing here is a dry
// run. It is also LONG-RUNNING — ten to twenty minutes — and this call returns
// as soon as the workflow starts, not when the cluster is usable.
//
// PAUSES ONCE FOR THE PERSON, right after the GKE cluster is created and before
// anything is installed on it: the grants the infrastructure's apps need and
// Inframan will not make (the CI/CD builder account, and for cloud Monitoring
// its read and write grants), as one script. get_infrastructure_status shows
// `state.phase` needs_setup; get_provisioning_setup gives the script. Show it
// to the person, wait for them to say they ran it, then call
// verify_provisioning_setup — a pass resumes provisioning on its own.
//
// ASK THE USER FIRST. Do not guess any of these; a wrong answer is not a wrong
// argument, it is a cluster that has to be torn down by hand:
// 1. `name` — what to call the environment, e.g. "prod" or "platform".
// 2. `region` — which GCP region, e.g. "asia-south1". This decides latency and
// price and cannot be changed afterwards.
// 3. `tag` — "prod" or "nonprod". prod gets a REGIONAL cluster (nodes across
// three zones) and Knative with scale-to-zero disabled; nonprod gets a zonal
// cluster and Spot nodes to save cost. This is a cost and availability
// decision, not a label.
// 4. `node_pools` — at least one APPLICATION pool, with a machine type and node
// bounds. This is the bulk of the bill.
// 5. CIDRs — ask whether they have an existing network these must not overlap.
// An overlapping range is refused by GCP well into the run and the failure is
// PERMANENT: retrying cannot fix it, the record has to be reset and provisioned
// again.
//
// Also call list_cloud_accounts first — `cloud_account_id` names the GCP
// project this is built in, and there is nothing to infer it from. Its
// effective_status must be verified: any other account is refused with the step
// that fixes it (needs_update: the user reruns its setup, then
// verify_cloud_account).
//
// node_pools is a JSON ARRAY, passed as a string. A complete example:
//
// node_pools =
// "[{\"name\":\"application-pool\",\"machine_type\":\"e2-standard-4\",\"min_node_count\":1,\"max_node_count\":3,\"initial_node_count\":1,\"disk_size_gb\":50,\"disk_type\":\"pd-balanced\",\"spot\":false}]"
//
// Only `name`, `machine_type` and `max_node_count` are required per pool; the
// rest default to 50GB pd-balanced, on-demand (non-Spot), with auto-repair and
// auto-upgrade on. The field names are exact — `min_node_count` and
// `max_node_count`, NOT min_nodes/max_nodes, which are silently read as zero.
//
// WHAT YOU DO NOT PASS: the system pool (the platform adds it; istiod and the
// ingress gateway are pinned to it by taint — the monitoring pool now belongs
// to the Observability app and appears only when that app is installed), the
// cluster name (generated), or which platform apps to install (the lean default
// set is fixed; Serverless and Observability are added later from the
// infrastructure's Apps tab in the console — a console operation).
//
// `zero_trust` (default false) is the security posture: Network Policy on the
// cluster, an Istio sidecar in every application pod, and per-service
// mTLS/authorization policies. It costs about one extra system node plus ~100m
// CPU per pod, and it is CREATE-TIME ONLY — Network Policy recreates node pools
// on a running cluster. Ask the user; do not default it on.
//
// `secret_manager` (default true) keeps the infrastructure's secrets —
// infrastructure secrets, secret env-group variables, the passwords of Redis,
// PostgreSQL, Grafana, LikeC4 and pgweb — in the cloud account's secret
// manager. Leave it on unless the user asks: off is only allowed for a
// zero-trust infrastructure on Google Cloud that the instance reaches across
// its hub, and is refused anywhere else (code `secret_manager_required`),
// because there the infrastructure's certificate authority is kept in Secret
// Manager. On, its grants join the cloud account's setup: an infrastructure
// read through its gateway is refused until the account holds them (the user
// reruns the account's setup, then verify_cloud_account); a zero-trust one is
// created, and what needs Secret Manager on it waits for the account — the
// answer's message says so. Off, the grants leave the account's setup unless
// another of its infrastructures has Secret Manager on.
//
// Node counts feed the pod-CIDR budget. GKE pre-allocates two IPs per pod per
// node, so the pod range has to cover the platform pools plus your pools at
// their maximum. A pod_cidr too small for the pools you asked for is refused up
// front with the arithmetic — widen the range or lower max_node_count rather
// than retrying.
//
// HOW MANY: a managed instance runs ONE infrastructure per cloud; on the
// central hosting there is no limit (get_infrastructure_limit says which). Call
// list_infrastructures first: on a managed instance a second on the same cloud
// is refused, and the answer is not to retry it.
//
// Afterwards, poll get_infrastructure_status until it reads READY. Do not
// create applications or attach domains before then. On an in-cluster
// infrastructure, READY may come with `build_identity`: builds need one command
// the user runs, and the first release waits on it.
//
// It needs the infrastructure:provision permission, changes state and is
// offered to agents and service accounts (surfaces: console, cli, agent).
func (c *Client) ProvisionInfrastructure(ctx context.Context, p ProvisionInfrastructureParams) (json.RawMessage, error) {
	return c.invoke(ctx, "provision_infrastructure", p.args())
}

