// SetBusinessHours calls set_business_hours: PUT
// /v1/capabilities/infrastructures/:infrastructure_id/business-hours.
//
// Schedule the hours an infrastructure stays up, sleeping it the rest of the
// week.
//
// Sets when this cluster's workloads stay running: the days, a start and a stop
// time, in a timezone. Inside those hours the workloads run; outside them the
// platform stops everything. The clock is the whole decision — whether anyone
// is using the cluster at the stop time does not change it. A build or deploy
// that is running, or finished within the last hour, holds the cluster up past
// its stop.
//
// The default is around the clock: no schedule, which is the same as every day
// from 00:00 to 24:00. A stop of "24:00" is midnight at the end of the day, so
// whole days work — weekdays "1,2,3,4,5" from 00:00 to 24:00 keeps the cluster
// up all week and asleep all weekend.
//
// WHAT SLEEPING ACTUALLY DOES: everything in the cluster stops — the customer's
// services of every kind (web, worker, Knative), the databases, the monitoring
// stack, CI/CD, the Knative control plane and the ingress gateway with its load
// balancer — and every node is released, the system pool's too. On AWS the NAT
// gateways are deleted, and on GCP a hub spoke is detached. Nothing answers
// until the cluster wakes: at the schedule's next start, when someone wakes it,
// or when a release starts — a request does not. Nothing is lost: disks, the
// load balancer's and the NAT's addresses, secrets and every Kubernetes object
// survive, so DNS records and allow-lists hold. What stops being billed is the
// nodes, the load balancer, the NAT gateways and a spoke; the control plane,
// disks and reserved addresses keep costing regardless.
//
// CONFIRM WITH THE USER BEFORE CALLING THIS, and confirm two things
// specifically: which environment it is, and that they accept everything on it
// — its web applications included — being down outside those hours until the
// schedule or a wake brings it back. This is a cost feature for development and
// staging clusters. Setting it on anything serving real traffic changes that
// traffic's behaviour on a timer, and the platform deliberately does not refuse
// to do so — plenty of clusters tagged prod are demo and internal environments
// — so the judgement is yours and the user's, not the platform's.
//
// TIMES ARE LOCAL TO THE TIMEZONE YOU PASS. "9 to 6" is a statement about a
// working day, so pass the user's IANA zone ("Asia/Kolkata", "Europe/Berlin",
// "America/New_York") rather than converting to UTC yourself — the schedule
// then follows daylight saving on its own.
//
// A window that crosses midnight is refused: pick the days you mean and keep
// start before stop, with 24:00 for a day that runs to midnight. Pass
// enabled=false to switch the schedule off; a cluster asleep at that moment is
// woken by the next scheduler tick rather than left down.
//
// This replaces the whole schedule rather than patching it: send every field
// you want kept.
//
// It needs the infrastructure:provision permission, changes state and is
// offered to agents and service accounts (surfaces: console, cli, agent).
func (c *Client) SetBusinessHours(ctx context.Context, p SetBusinessHoursParams) (json.RawMessage, error) {
	return c.invoke(ctx, "set_business_hours", p.args())
}

