// UpdateRevisionSetting calls update_revision_setting: PATCH
// /v1/capabilities/applications/:application_id/revision-setting.
//
// Choose which revision serves an application: latest, a fixed one, or a split.
// THIS IS HOW YOU ROLL BACK.
//
// Decides which REVISION users reach. A revision is an immutable version of the
// whole application — every service together — so this moves people between
// versions of the application, never between services.
//
// THIS IS ROLLBACK. There is no separate rollback tool and no snapshot to
// restore: going back means pointing traffic at an earlier revision with
// mode="fixed". Read the revision list, take the id of the last one that was
// good, and send it. Its Knative services are redeployed from the manifest that
// release stored, traffic moves to it, and every revision left with no traffic
// has its Knative services removed — traffic FIRST, so nothing is torn down
// while it is still serving.
//
// Going forward again is the same call: mode="latest", or fixed on a newer id.
// Nothing was destroyed on the way back, which is why this is safe to do while
// you are still working out what broke.
//
// Three modes, and picking the right one is the whole job:
// • `latest` — the newest deployed revision serves everything, and keeps
// serving as new ones land. The normal state.
// • `fixed` — one named revision serves everything and STAYS there even when
// newer ones deploy. Use it to hold a known-good version while something is
// investigated.
// • `split` — weighted across two or more revisions, for a canary. Refused for
// an application whose services keep a disk (storage:): one disk holds one
// revision's version at a time.
//
// `revision_id` is required for `fixed`. `splits` is required for `split`, as
// `revision_id=percent` comma separated and summing to exactly 100 — e.g.
// `a1b2c3d4-...=90,e5f6a7b8-...=10`. Neither is needed for `latest`: it
// resolves the newest deployed revision itself, so do not try to find the id
// yourself.
//
// Get ids from list_application_revisions, newest first. A revision that failed
// to deploy cannot take traffic, and one belonging to another application is
// rejected.
//
// IT RETURNS BEFORE THE CLUSTER HAS CAUGHT UP. The setting is queued for the
// application's deploy queue, which changes the application one request at a
// time: after a release that is deploying, never beside another change. In its
// turn it stores the weights, brings back a revision's workload if it has to,
// moves the routes, and deletes what no longer serves once the gateway routes
// by them — seconds, or minutes behind a release or when pods wait on cluster
// capacity. Do NOT send the setting again to check — that queues a second
// change. Watch list_application_activity instead: `deploy_plan` is the change
// starting, with what it will do, and `traffic_settled` is the cluster having
// caught up. Everything the platform does lands there, so it is the one place
// to look rather than polling the revision list and comparing fields yourself.
//
// This moves real users immediately. It is reversible: send another setting and
// nothing has been overwritten — the revision you left is still a revision.
//
// Internal service-to-service calls are unaffected: they address a pinned
// version directly and deliberately bypass this.
//
// It needs the application:deploy permission, changes state and is offered to
// agents and service accounts (surfaces: console, cli, agent).
func (c *Client) UpdateRevisionSetting(ctx context.Context, p UpdateRevisionSettingParams) (json.RawMessage, error) {
	return c.invoke(ctx, "update_revision_setting", p.args())
}

