// VerifyCloudIdentity calls verify_cloud_identity: POST
// /v1/capabilities/infrastructures/:infrastructure_id/addons/:app_id/cloud-identity/verify.
//
// Check an add-on's cloud identity is set up, by reading its IAM policy as the
// cloud account.
//
// On an AWS infrastructure it reads the tier's IAM role back as the account's
// role instead, and judges its trust: EKS Pod Identity, this cluster, the
// tier's namespace. Each install is handed the role as it is created.
//
// Applies the add-on's namespace and Kubernetes service account in the cluster,
// then reads the tier's GCP service account as the cloud account: it must
// exist, grant roles/iam.serviceAccountUser to the cloud account and
// roles/iam.workloadIdentityUser to the tier's pods, and hold any project role
// the app declares. Call it after the user tells you they have run the commands
// from get_cloud_identity_setup.
//
// WHY A READ. The cloud account's own ceremony grants it a small IAM reader
// role, so Inframan can act as it and read exactly what the ceremony set, in
// seconds, and name the grant that is missing. It reads the policy rather than
// obtaining a token through it, so a grant made moments ago can be in the
// policy and still be settling.
//
// WHAT A PASS PROVES, AND WHAT IT DOES NOT. It proves the account exists and
// carries the bindings the ceremony makes, so every install of this tier runs
// under them. It does NOT prove the identity can read or write anything else,
// and it says nothing about any other tier. Do not report it as "backups are
// working"; report it as "the identity is established". The response and
// get_cloud_identity_setup both carry a `proves` field saying this; prefer its
// wording to your own.
//
// Returns as soon as the read starts. It takes seconds — re-read
// get_cloud_identity_setup for the verdict, where a failure arrives as
// `last_error` naming the missing grant. A failure is almost always that the
// commands have not actually been run yet; show the user `last_error` rather
// than guessing.
//
// Safe to call more than once, and safe to call before the user has done
// anything — the worst case is a failed probe that tells them so. It creates
// nothing billable: a namespace the app is going to need anyway, the tier's
// service account, and a Job that deletes itself.
//
// It needs the infrastructure:provision permission, changes state and is
// offered to agents and service accounts (surfaces: console, cli, agent).
